.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO 3 "2026-07-11" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do \- perform an HTTP request and buffer the full response
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do(chttpcli cli,
                             const chttp_request_t *req,
                             chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Acquires a pool slot (blocking if all slots are in use), executes the
HTTP request synchronously, buffers the entire response body, and
stores a heap-allocated chttpcli_response in *resp_out.

The caller owns *resp_out on success and must call
chttpclient_resp_free when done.

Redirects (301, 302, 303, 307, 308) are followed automatically, up to
50 hops.  301/302/303 rewrite the method to a bodyless GET (HEAD is
left as HEAD); 307/308 preserve the original method and body.  The
Location header may be an absolute URL, a protocol-relative
reference ("//host/path"), an absolute-path reference ("/foo"), or a
general relative reference ("foo", "../foo", "./foo", "?query");
all are resolved per RFC 3986.  Dot-segment normalization
("..", ".") only ever rewrites the path component; a query string
is always carried forward byte-for-byte, even one that happens to
contain "/", "..", or "." characters.  If the request URL embedded
credentials (see below), the resulting Authorization header is
resent on every hop that stays on the same origin (scheme, host,
and port) and is dropped permanently the first time a hop changes
origin.

The request URL accepts http://, https://, and http+unix:// (Unix
domain socket support is identical across every tier; the
encoded-socket-path form is "http+unix://%2Fpath%2Fto%2Fsock/path",
matching Python's requests-unixsocket convention).  Both a plain
hostname/IPv4 literal
and a bracketed IPv6 literal ("https://[::1]:8443/path") are accepted
for the network forms.  A URL may embed credentials
("http://user:pass@host/path"); they are turned into an
Authorization: Basic ... header automatically unless the request
already sets its own Authorization header (not applicable to
http+unix://, which has no userinfo convention).  A trailing
"#fragment" is recognized and discarded (fragments are never sent to
a server).

If req->expect_continue is true and the request has a body, only the
request's headers are sent first, followed by up to a one second
wait for the server's response:

  - If the server sends a "100 Continue" interim response, the body
    is sent and the real final response is read normally.
  - If the server answers with a final status directly instead
    (e.g. to reject the request without wanting the body), that
    response is delivered to the caller as-is and the body is never
    sent.
  - If the one second wait elapses with no response at all, the
    body is sent anyway and the final response is read normally
    (the server is assumed not to support the mechanism).

expect_continue has no effect on a request with no body, and is
currently honored only by chttpclient_do and chttpclient_do_streaming
(Tier 1); chttpclient_do_async and everything built on it ignore it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       Client handle; must not be NULL.
req       Request to execute; must not be NULL.
resp_out  Output parameter; must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success
       Request completed; *resp_out is valid.

ccol_invalid_args
       Any argument is NULL.

ccol_not_enough_memory
       Memory allocation failed.

ccol_timed_out
       Request or connect timeout triggered.

ccol_not_permitted
       Client is being destroyed; no new requests are accepted.

ccol_http_invalid_url
       URL is malformed, uses an unsupported scheme (only http://,
       https://, and http+unix:// are supported), has a missing/
       invalid host, port, or userinfo component, or (for
       http+unix://) names a socket path too long to fit
       sockaddr_un.sun_path.

ccol_http_host_resolution_failed
       DNS resolution failed for the target host (never returned for
       http+unix://, which has no DNS step).

ccol_http_connection_failed
       The connection could not be established (e.g. connection
       refused).

ccol_http_too_many_redirects
       The redirect chain exceeded 50 hops.

ccol_http_tls_handshake_failed
       The TLS handshake failed for a reason other than certificate
       verification.

ccol_http_tls_cert_verification_failed
       The peer certificate or hostname could not be verified, or the
       configured client certificate/key/CA bundle path was not
       readable.

ccol_http_transfer_aborted
       The connection failed mid-transfer, the server sent a
       malformed HTTP/1.1 response, or a streaming chttpcli_write_fn
       returned fewer bytes than it was given.

ccol_unexpected_failure
       Any other internal failure not covered by the above codes.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_do_streaming (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_async (3),
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttpclient_resp_free (3)
